{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791210084,"nonce":"hZLnauDwo/i+xYKuvM/4jSsoT1U=","title":"LLVM promotion clones every module and leaks its context, IR and engine","message":"compile_llvm_bead (tiered_backend.rs), per promotion:\n- `live.clone()` copies every loaded IrModule (stdlib included) to compile one root;\n- `Box::leak(Box::new(Context::create()))` and `Box::leak(Box::new(backend))` keep the LLVM context, the module IR and the MCJIT engine for the rest of the run, though only the installed code is used afterwards;\n- add_unserved_callees adds the root's whole static call closure with no bound.\n\nash fixed the same three shapes recently: d6f064e (modules share the decoded program instead of copying it), 2d490e0 (free a promotion's IR once its code is loaded), bebe948 (at most 64 callee bodies per promotion).\n\nMeasured on the nuc at c8147a87 (benchmark_runner --single-target, RSS sampled every 20ms with RAYZOR_PROFILE_TIER_EVENTS=1): each kernel promotes exactly once, early in warmup (+0.03..0.09s), compile-\u003einstall 0.12-0.42s; tiered peak RSS vs cranelift: nbody 90 vs 77MB, fibonacci 84 vs 70, mandelbrot 172 vs 160, deltablue 172 vs 165. So on the benchmark kernels promotion costs ~10-15MB once and is outside the measured runs — not what limits their speed. binarytrees peaks at 2.8-4.6GB, but that is its per-iteration leak (benchmark_runner notes it), not promotion.\n\nThe cost scales with promotions x program size, which matters once promotion fires more than once per run (see the CLI no-promotion issue).","files":null}]}